<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Idrija</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Idrija is a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, the site of one of the world's largest mercury mines, discovered around 1490 and worked for some five centuries. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> from 1509 it was a major source of royal revenue, and its mercury was used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> to refine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> by amalgamation. The mine closed in the 1990s, and together with Almadén in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/idrija.jpg" alt="Idrija" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luka Peternel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Idrija-view-2025-Luka-Peternel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
